{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat draft_reference_gaps.md && printf '\\\\n--- GAP JSON ---\\\\n' && cat draft_reference_gaps.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nOne targeted retrieval pass is justified to position the adaptive ILR ellipsoids against joint multivariate conformal-region methods; the remaining citation coverage does not warrant expansion.\n\n## Basis and Scope\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, `references_provenance.json`, `claims_grounding.json`, `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The draft cites all 25 existing bibliography entries, every cited key has a bibliography entry, and the provenance registry covers the same keys. This checks citation presence, not independent full-text verification. The 47 claim-grounding entries distinguish retained numerical evidence, implementation evidence, and literature support. The strategy centers a condition-dependent empirical comparison, not a new conformal guarantee. Global memory was used only as qualitative context.\n\n## Missing Literature\n\nJoint multivariate conformal prediction regions with adaptive residual scale or ellipsoidal geometry.\n\n## Target: related-work-joint-region-comparators\n\n- **Section:** Related Work, existing “Calibration and adaptation” paragraph.\n- **Priority:** high.\n- **Citation intent:** positioning.\n- **Anchor:** “our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling”.\n- **Reason:** The paragraph moves from scalar conformalized quantile regression (`Romano2019`) to the paper's joint multivariate construction without directly citing the closest joint-region family. `Lei2018` supports general split-conformal regression; `Stone1974`, `Geisser1975`, and `Barber2021Jackknife` address residual reuse and distinct calibration constructions. The covariance-regularization citations in Method support shape stabilization, not positioning against multivariate conformal regions. Neither the existing bibliography nor its provenance identifies a dedicated joint multivariate conformal-region comparator. This is a reviewer-facing gap because adaptive residual geometry is central to the manuscript's question.\n- **Focused search topics:** “split-conformal multivariate regression ellipsoidal prediction regions residual covariance”; “locally adaptive conformal multivariate prediction regions covariate-dependent residual scaling”.\n- **Bounded follow-up:** Seek 1–3 directly relevant sources and add a brief comparison in this paragraph distinguishing joint-region geometry, adaptation, and separate calibration. Check each source's actual construction before asserting similarity. Do not add experimental baselines, claim superiority over untested methods, or imply that any specific prior method uses the paper's ILR transform or boundary routing. These are retrieval topics, not already verified references.\n\n## No Additional Targets\n\n- Composition geometry and logistic-normal background already have direct citations to `AitchisonShen1980`, `Aitchison1982`, `Egozcue2003`, and `Pawlowsky2011`; their presence does not justify another broad compositional-literature pass.\n- Marginal versus conditional validity, classification distinctions, and covariate-shift corrections already have direct citations in Related Work. The standard calibration argument is also cited in Method.\n- Ridge fitting, covariance regularization, Wilson intervals, and bootstrap uncertainty already have method-specific citations. Implementation details are grounded in source artifacts rather than treated as unsupported literature claims.\n- Reported coverage, volume, controls, and limitations are grounded in retained study evidence. They do not require external citations merely because they are quantitative or unfavorable.\n- Real-data applications, structural zeros, alternative transforms, Hellinger scores, and extra calibration schemes would expand the story beyond the evaluated study and are not retrieval targets.\n\nNo references were retrieved. `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `references_provenance.json` remain unchanged. There is no analysis failure (`failure_reason` is empty).\n\n--- GAP JSON ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"One targeted retrieval pass is justified to position the adaptive ILR ellipsoids against joint multivariate conformal-region methods; the remaining citation coverage does not warrant expansion.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Joint multivariate conformal prediction regions with adaptive residual scale or ellipsoidal geometry\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"related-work-joint-region-comparators\",\n      \"section\": \"Related Work\",\n      \"priority\": \"high\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"positioning\",\n      \"reason\": \"The Calibration and adaptation paragraph cites scalar conformalized quantile regression before contrasting it with joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling, but supplies no direct citation for the joint multivariate region family closest to the studied adaptive ellipsoids. The existing bibliography and provenance cover general conformal calibration, quantile intervals, cross-validation, and covariance shrinkage, without identifying a dedicated joint multivariate conformal-region comparator. One to three directly relevant sources would address this central positioning gap without changing the empirical comparisons or implying algorithmic novelty.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"split-conformal multivariate regression ellipsoidal prediction regions residual covariance\",\n        \"locally adaptive conformal multivariate prediction regions covariate-dependent residual scaling\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*reference_query_plan*' -g '*SKILL.md' -g 'paper.tex' -g 'references*' -g 'global_memory_summary.md' -g 'memory.md' -g '*schema*'; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"pwd; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*reference_query_plan*' -g '*SKILL.md' -g 'paper.tex' -g 'references*' -g 'global_memory_summary.md' -g 'memory.md' -g '*schema*'; printf '\\\\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\\\\n'; cat .codex/skills/memory-log/SKILL.md; printf '\\\\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- SUMMARY ---\\\\n'; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspace/paper\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261107_055810.log\nglobal_memory_summary.md\npaper.tex\nreference_query_plan.json\nreferences.bib.prov.json\nreferences_provenance.json.prov.json\nreference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreferences_provenance.json\nscientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json\nscientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json\nmemory.md\nscientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json\nreferences.bib\nscientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\nreference_query_plan.md\nreference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261107_055810.log\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\nreference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.json\npaper_diagnostics/reference_query_plan/round_0000/attempt_001/reference_query_plan.md\n\n--- MEMORY SKILL ---\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n--- PLAN SCHEMA INSTANCE ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Coverage and common-simplex-volume efficiency of externally split-calibrated, training-adaptive ILR ellipsoids for strictly positive continuous compositional regression across synthetic noise, boundary, and covariate-shift regimes.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Independent split-conformal calibration at nominal joint coverage 0.90\",\n    \"Isometric log-ratio (ILR) representation with quadratic-feature ridge location and log-residual variance regression\",\n    \"Deterministic four-fold out-of-fold location residuals with an all-training-row final location refit\",\n    \"Training-fixed predicted-boundary tertiles with pooled and group-shrunk residual second-moment shapes\",\n    \"Positive-definite, input-adaptive ILR ellipsoids returned through Cholesky factors\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Evaluated: Euclidean residual balls intersected with the simplex, using clipped and renormalized composition-space ridge predictions\",\n    \"Evaluated: isotropic ILR residual balls, equivalent to Aitchison-distance regions\",\n    \"Evaluated variants: single 75/25 location/residual split versus four-fold residual fitting; hard boundary-tertile routing versus continuous convex covariance interpolation\",\n    \"Sanity controls, not learned baselines: point-copy singleton, full simplex, constant inputs, and training-pair derangement\",\n    \"Literature-only positioning: conditional versus marginal coverage and weighted conformal inference under covariate shift; neither groupwise calibration nor importance weighting was implemented\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Local synthetic C1: K=3 homoskedastic logistic-normal regression with five Gaussian covariates\",\n    \"Local synthetic C2: K=3 covariate-dependent heteroskedastic logistic-normal regression\",\n    \"Local synthetic C3: K=8 near-boundary Dirichlet regression with strictly positive responses\",\n    \"Local synthetic C4: K=8 Dirichlet regression with test-only covariate location and scale shift\",\n    \"Matched C2-derived controls: C5 constant covariates and C6 deranged training covariate-response pairing; these are not independent datasets\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"Representation comparison: direct simplex-coordinate Euclidean balls versus isotropic ILR balls versus covariate-adaptive ILR ellipsoids; all use small quadratic-feature ridge predictors, not neural architectures\",\n    \"Residual-estimation change: one 75/25 training split replaced by four-fold out-of-fold residuals and a full-training location refit; training-only changes do not replace independent conformal calibration\",\n    \"Shape-routing change: hard training-frozen boundary tertiles versus continuous convex interpolation of training-estimated positive-definite covariance shapes; search evidence does not establish a reliable interpolation advantage\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"The four-fold residual variant has the highest recorded search utility; its comparison with the split variant also changes location training size and is not a mechanism-isolating ablation\",\n    \"Final paired seed evidence favors adaptive ILR ellipsoids over isotropic ILR balls in both coverage and volume, but the Euclidean baseline has smaller average volume and no resolved coverage difference\",\n    \"Regime dependence matters: near-boundary C3 regions occupy nearly the full simplex, while shifted C4 coverage is below the nominal target; no universal efficiency or shift-robustness claim is supported\",\n    \"Marginal coverage and descriptive covariate-stratum coverage differ; the recorded zero worst-stratum summary includes empty strata under constant-input controls and is not evidence of observed zero coverage in a populated group\",\n    \"Efficiency must be compared using shared uniform-simplex probes, not raw volumes in different coordinate systems; Wilson intervals, paired seed bootstrap intervals, and integration errors address different uncertainty sources, with only three search and five final seeds\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Distribution-Free Predictive Inference For Regression\\\" Lei\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Starter-listed foundation for independent split calibration, finite-sample rank selection, and marginal rather than conditional guarantees; the run uses an externally calibrated 0.90 joint-coverage threshold. This is a known-title lookup, not a search for the prohibited task-specific paper.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"isometric log-ratio transformation Aitchison distance compositional data\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Grounds the actual ILR representation and isotropic log-ratio baseline against Euclidean simplex geometry. The starter-listed Aitchison background is relevant; no Hellinger geometry or exact-zero extension was evaluated. Keep this representation-only query separate from conformal terminology.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"multivariate split conformal prediction Mahalanobis ellipsoidal regions\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Positions the implemented vector-valued standardized residual norm and Cholesky-shaped prediction regions against general multivariate conformal regression, without searching the prohibited composition-specific evaluation topic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"out-of-fold residuals heteroskedastic regression variance estimation\",\n      \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Targets the highest-utility search variant's four-fold residual construction and all-row variance fit. Relevant work should distinguish training-only residual estimation from CV+, jackknife+, and cross-conformal calibration, which were not implemented. The simultaneous location refit prevents isolated causal attribution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"covariance shrinkage positive definite heteroskedastic multivariate regression\",\n      \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Grounds the pooled/group residual second-moment shrinkage and stable positive-definite shapes shared by the strongest variants. Continuous covariance interpolation is a secondary evaluated variation, not an established improvement or a new neural architecture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"logistic-normal Dirichlet regression compositional responses boundary\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The actual benchmark consists only of local positive-composition logistic-normal and Dirichlet generators with K=3 or K=8. These model families clarify heteroskedasticity and near-boundary behavior more precisely than local case IDs or unused public dataset names. Do not add conformal terminology to this query.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"distribution-free predictive inference marginal conditional coverage limitations\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Explains why one pooled conformal threshold and input-adaptive shapes do not provide subgroup guarantees. The released sign-stratum diagnostics are descriptive, and empty control strata must not be interpreted as populated groups with zero coverage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Conformal Prediction Under Covariate Shift\\\" Tibshirani\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Starter-listed comparator literature for the explicit test-only covariate shift in C4 and its below-target observed coverage. Weighted conformal inference is a related-work alternative, not an evaluated baseline or a guarantee enjoyed by the unweighted method.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"multivariate prediction regions volume efficiency Monte Carlo integration\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Grounds evaluation by common-measure region volume rather than incomparable coordinate-space volumes. Shared uniform-simplex probes yield paired Monte Carlo errors, distinct from finite-test coverage intervals and seed uncertainty; near-full-simplex C3 volumes make efficiency limitations scientifically relevant.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify, guess, retrieve, or inspect the prohibited task-specific paper, its authors, title, results, mirrors, abstracts, reviews, presentations, repositories, or pages citing, discussing, or reproducing it\",\n    \"Broad task-identifying combinations such as conformal prediction compositional outputs reliability evaluation, conformal compositional benchmark, or conformal simplex comparison; keep general conformal methods and composition-only representation/generator searches separate\",\n    \"Citation chaining, cited-by searches, related-paper expansion, or aggregation and review pages that could expose prohibited material; begin from allowed starter-title lookups and narrowly scoped methods only, and stop immediately if prohibited content is encountered\",\n    \"CIFAR-10, image-classifier APS/RAPS, softmax networks, transformers, or other public datasets and neural architectures absent from the executed experiments\",\n    \"Hellinger conformal comparison, smoothed-log-ratio experiments, structural-zero robustness, or probability/credal-set coverage inferred from categorical labels; these were not implemented\",\n    \"Queries presenting groupwise calibration, Mondrian calibration, multivalid coverage, jackknife+, CV+, or importance-weighted conformal as executed methods; these may only be explicitly distinguished as unevaluated alternatives if needed\",\n    \"Universal superiority, guaranteed conditional coverage, demonstrated interpolation improvement, or covariate-shift robustness as established findings\",\n    \"Generic optimization, benchmarking best practices, scientific method, paper-writing advice, campaign chronology, internal attempt identifiers, and local C1-C6 identifiers as standalone literature queries\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n--- SUMMARY ---\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-07 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Boundary-Stratified Hellinger Reliability Audit proposed; status=idea_bundle_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=An untested proposal and unavailable source provide no evaluated finding warranting a distilled recommendation change.\n  - evidence: Bootstrap manifest reports correct=true and combined_score=0.8770158838342736; no recent ideas or attempts. Named incumbent program_0/src/program.py absent; no implementation defect inferred. No task program, evaluator, or experiment executed. Matching rubric arrays and one canonical local memory path were constructed directly.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T05:46:16.389001+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.8776261506257234, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.8776261506257234, \"correct\": true}\n- 2026-11-07 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Cross-Fitted ILR Residual Geometry proposed; status=idea_bundle_ready. gate=updated; trigger=b; reason=The inspected candidate contract establishes an actionable interface-feasibility category not yet covered in the empty distilled conclusions.\n  - evidence: Prior correct=true and combined_score=0.8776261506257234 coexist with rubric_overall_pass=false, with no specific failed criterion available. Named relative candidate source and IMPLEMENTATION.md document positive compositions, five covariates, ILR-only location/Cholesky outputs, evaluator-owned calibration, and a 75/25 location/residual split. No candidate, evaluator, experiment, computational probe, test, or outside literature retrieval was performed. Required JSON rubric arrays and executable snippet counts were reviewed directly.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T05:51:40.737730+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.8818756857036072, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.8818756857036072, \"correct\": true}\n- 2026-11-07 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Continuous Boundary Covariance Interpolation proposed; status=idea_bundle_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=This untested proposal stays within the documented shape controls and supplies no evaluated finding or interface change requiring a distilled recommendation update.\n  - evidence: Named cycle02 candidate source and IMPLEMENTATION.md confirm training-only four-fold ILR residuals, all-row location refitting, and hard boundary-tertile factor routing. Cycle02 recorded combined_score=0.8818756857036072, correct=true, rubric_overall_pass=true; cycle01 recorded combined_score=0.8776261506257234, correct=true, rubric_overall_pass=false without a criterion-level cause. Single aggregate evaluations provide no replicated superiority or mechanism attribution. No candidate, evaluator, scientific experiment, test, computational probe, package installation, or external retrieval was performed. Required JSON arrays and executable pattern counts 6, 6, 7 were reviewed statically; each pattern is below 1024 characters.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-07T05:57:23.686885+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.8818118342646846, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 0.8818118342646846, \"correct\": true}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
