{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"printf '\\\\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\\\\n'; find .. -name AGENTS.md -print; printf '\\\\n--- QUERY PLAN ---\\\\n'; cat reference_query_plan.json; printf '\\\\n--- DRAFT ---\\\\n'; sed -n '1,240p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"\n--- INSTRUCTIONS ---\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_044438_1794026678607719312/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_044438_1794026678607719312/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_044438_1794026678607719312/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_044438_1794026678607719312/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_044438_1794026678607719312/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_044438_1794026678607719312/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261107_044438_1794026678607719312/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n\n--- QUERY PLAN ---\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Coverage and common-simplex-volume efficiency of externally split-calibrated, training-adaptive ILR ellipsoids for strictly positive continuous compositional regression across synthetic noise, boundary, and covariate-shift regimes.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Independent split-conformal calibration at nominal joint coverage 0.90\",\n    \"Isometric log-ratio (ILR) representation with quadratic-feature ridge location and log-residual variance regression\",\n    \"Deterministic four-fold out-of-fold location residuals with an all-training-row final location refit\",\n    \"Training-fixed predicted-boundary tertiles with pooled and group-shrunk residual second-moment shapes\",\n    \"Positive-definite, input-adaptive ILR ellipsoids returned through Cholesky factors\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Evaluated: Euclidean residual balls intersected with the simplex, using clipped and renormalized composition-space ridge predictions\",\n    \"Evaluated: isotropic ILR residual balls, equivalent to Aitchison-distance regions\",\n    \"Evaluated variants: single 75/25 location/residual split versus four-fold residual fitting; hard boundary-tertile routing versus continuous convex covariance interpolation\",\n    \"Sanity controls, not learned baselines: point-copy singleton, full simplex, constant inputs, and training-pair derangement\",\n    \"Literature-only positioning: conditional versus marginal coverage and weighted conformal inference under covariate shift; neither groupwise calibration nor importance weighting was implemented\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Local synthetic C1: K=3 homoskedastic logistic-normal regression with five Gaussian covariates\",\n    \"Local synthetic C2: K=3 covariate-dependent heteroskedastic logistic-normal regression\",\n    \"Local synthetic C3: K=8 near-boundary Dirichlet regression with strictly positive responses\",\n    \"Local synthetic C4: K=8 Dirichlet regression with test-only covariate location and scale shift\",\n    \"Matched C2-derived controls: C5 constant covariates and C6 deranged training covariate-response pairing; these are not independent datasets\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"Representation comparison: direct simplex-coordinate Euclidean balls versus isotropic ILR balls versus covariate-adaptive ILR ellipsoids; all use small quadratic-feature ridge predictors, not neural architectures\",\n    \"Residual-estimation change: one 75/25 training split replaced by four-fold out-of-fold residuals and a full-training location refit; training-only changes do not replace independent conformal calibration\",\n    \"Shape-routing change: hard training-frozen boundary tertiles versus continuous convex interpolation of training-estimated positive-definite covariance shapes; search evidence does not establish a reliable interpolation advantage\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"The four-fold residual variant has the highest recorded search utility; its comparison with the split variant also changes location training size and is not a mechanism-isolating ablation\",\n    \"Final paired seed evidence favors adaptive ILR ellipsoids over isotropic ILR balls in both coverage and volume, but the Euclidean baseline has smaller average volume and no resolved coverage difference\",\n    \"Regime dependence matters: near-boundary C3 regions occupy nearly the full simplex, while shifted C4 coverage is below the nominal target; no universal efficiency or shift-robustness claim is supported\",\n    \"Marginal coverage and descriptive covariate-stratum coverage differ; the recorded zero worst-stratum summary includes empty strata under constant-input controls and is not evidence of observed zero coverage in a populated group\",\n    \"Efficiency must be compared using shared uniform-simplex probes, not raw volumes in different coordinate systems; Wilson intervals, paired seed bootstrap intervals, and integration errors address different uncertainty sources, with only three search and five final seeds\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Distribution-Free Predictive Inference For Regression\\\" Lei\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Starter-listed foundation for independent split calibration, finite-sample rank selection, and marginal rather than conditional guarantees; the run uses an externally calibrated 0.90 joint-coverage threshold. This is a known-title lookup, not a search for the prohibited task-specific paper.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"isometric log-ratio transformation Aitchison distance compositional data\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Grounds the actual ILR representation and isotropic log-ratio baseline against Euclidean simplex geometry. The starter-listed Aitchison background is relevant; no Hellinger geometry or exact-zero extension was evaluated. Keep this representation-only query separate from conformal terminology.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"multivariate split conformal prediction Mahalanobis ellipsoidal regions\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Positions the implemented vector-valued standardized residual norm and Cholesky-shaped prediction regions against general multivariate conformal regression, without searching the prohibited composition-specific evaluation topic.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"out-of-fold residuals heteroskedastic regression variance estimation\",\n      \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Targets the highest-utility search variant's four-fold residual construction and all-row variance fit. Relevant work should distinguish training-only residual estimation from CV+, jackknife+, and cross-conformal calibration, which were not implemented. The simultaneous location refit prevents isolated causal attribution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"covariance shrinkage positive definite heteroskedastic multivariate regression\",\n      \"category\": \"architectural_shift\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Grounds the pooled/group residual second-moment shrinkage and stable positive-definite shapes shared by the strongest variants. Continuous covariance interpolation is a secondary evaluated variation, not an established improvement or a new neural architecture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"logistic-normal Dirichlet regression compositional responses boundary\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The actual benchmark consists only of local positive-composition logistic-normal and Dirichlet generators with K=3 or K=8. These model families clarify heteroskedasticity and near-boundary behavior more precisely than local case IDs or unused public dataset names. Do not add conformal terminology to this query.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"distribution-free predictive inference marginal conditional coverage limitations\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Explains why one pooled conformal threshold and input-adaptive shapes do not provide subgroup guarantees. The released sign-stratum diagnostics are descriptive, and empty control strata must not be interpreted as populated groups with zero coverage.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Conformal Prediction Under Covariate Shift\\\" Tibshirani\",\n      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Starter-listed comparator literature for the explicit test-only covariate shift in C4 and its below-target observed coverage. Weighted conformal inference is a related-work alternative, not an evaluated baseline or a guarantee enjoyed by the unweighted method.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"multivariate prediction regions volume efficiency Monte Carlo integration\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Grounds evaluation by common-measure region volume rather than incomparable coordinate-space volumes. Shared uniform-simplex probes yield paired Monte Carlo errors, distinct from finite-test coverage intervals and seed uncertainty; near-full-simplex C3 volumes make efficiency limitations scientifically relevant.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any attempt to identify, guess, retrieve, or inspect the prohibited task-specific paper, its authors, title, results, mirrors, abstracts, reviews, presentations, repositories, or pages citing, discussing, or reproducing it\",\n    \"Broad task-identifying combinations such as conformal prediction compositional outputs reliability evaluation, conformal compositional benchmark, or conformal simplex comparison; keep general conformal methods and composition-only representation/generator searches separate\",\n    \"Citation chaining, cited-by searches, related-paper expansion, or aggregation and review pages that could expose prohibited material; begin from allowed starter-title lookups and narrowly scoped methods only, and stop immediately if prohibited content is encountered\",\n    \"CIFAR-10, image-classifier APS/RAPS, softmax networks, transformers, or other public datasets and neural architectures absent from the executed experiments\",\n    \"Hellinger conformal comparison, smoothed-log-ratio experiments, structural-zero robustness, or probability/credal-set coverage inferred from categorical labels; these were not implemented\",\n    \"Queries presenting groupwise calibration, Mondrian calibration, multivalid coverage, jackknife+, CV+, or importance-weighted conformal as executed methods; these may only be explicitly distinguished as unevaluated alternatives if needed\",\n    \"Universal superiority, guaranteed conditional coverage, demonstrated interpolation improvement, or covariate-shift robustness as established findings\",\n    \"Generic optimization, benchmarking best practices, scientific method, paper-writing advice, campaign chronology, internal attempt identifiers, and local C1-C6 identifiers as standalone literature queries\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n\n--- DRAFT ---\n\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,longtable,array,graphicx,subcaption}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Adaptive ILR Ellipsoids: Coverage--Volume Trade-offs on the Simplex}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\\newcommand{\\simplex}{\\Delta^{K-1}_{+}}\n\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n\\newcommand{\\clip}{\\operatorname{clip}}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPrediction regions for continuous compositions must be evaluated both for joint coverage and for their size on the simplex. We study whether training-adaptive isometric log-ratio (ILR) ellipsoids offer a useful coverage--volume trade-off relative to Euclidean and isotropic ILR balls. The adaptive configuration estimates residual scales and boundary-dependent shapes using training data, while an independent split-conformal calibration determines the region threshold. Across five held-out seeds and six synthetic conditions, heteroskedastic logistic-normal responses yield mean coverage 0.9044 and simplex-volume fraction 0.1476, compared with 0.9030 and 0.3336 for Euclidean balls. The benefit is not uniform: boundary-concentrated Dirichlet responses require volume fraction 0.9759, while covariate-shifted coverage falls to 0.8633 against a 0.90 target. Descriptive sign-stratum coverage also remains uneven. These findings support condition-dependent evaluation of region geometry, not universal superiority or conditional validity. Common-measure integration and separate uncertainty summaries expose failures that a single aggregate objective would obscure.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nCompositional responses describe how a whole is distributed among its parts: each component is nonnegative and the components sum to one. Uncertainty about an observed composition is therefore a joint, constrained prediction problem. An interval for every component does not by itself specify a calibrated joint region, and a region that covers almost the entire simplex can be reliable yet uninformative. This distinction matters even when the point predictor is accurate. We consider continuous composition-valued responses, not the discrete label of a classifier or an unobserved population probability vector.\n\nConformal prediction separates the quality of a fitted predictor from the calibration of its prediction region \\citep{Vovk2005,Papadopoulos2002,Angelopoulos2021}. Under exchangeability, a calibration sample supplies a finite-sample marginal coverage guarantee for a frozen score. That guarantee does not choose the score geometry, ensure small regions, or establish coverage at particular covariate values. Compositional geometry introduces an additional choice: distances in raw proportion coordinates and distances in log-ratio coordinates need not describe similarly sized subsets of the simplex \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003}. Comparing calibrated radii across these spaces is consequently insufficient.\n\nOur question is whether a training-adaptive ILR residual geometry yields smaller prediction regions at comparable observed joint coverage, and which conditions defeat that trade-off. We study a specific configuration that combines held-out training residuals, covariate-dependent diagonal scales, and training-defined boundary groups. Its threshold is calibrated independently, using the same split-conformal rule as two simpler reference geometries. All regions are evaluated by membership and by the same normalized simplex-volume measure. This makes the comparison meaningful even when their native coordinate systems differ.\n\nThe empirical contribution is deliberately scoped. We provide a matched evaluation of the three region families across homoskedastic, heteroskedastic, boundary-concentrated, and shifted synthetic responses, together with information-removal controls. We expose the distinction between integration error, finite-test uncertainty, and between-seed dispersion. We also retain a direct comparison of alternative adaptive training configurations, while explicitly declining to attribute bundled changes to cross-fitting alone. The central finding is a useful heteroskedastic trade-off accompanied by substantial boundary inefficiency and persistent shifted undercoverage; it is not a new universal conformal guarantee.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L90-L131]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/geometry_calibration_overview.png}\n\\caption{Study overview. Training data determine the location and geometry; independent calibration determines a single threshold. Euclidean balls, ILR balls, and adaptive ILR ellipsoids are compared by joint response coverage and a common simplex-volume measure. Boundary groups adapt shape, not calibration. Any simplex icon is conceptual, not a measured response cloud.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Composition-valued prediction.}\nLogistic-normal models describe continuous distributions on the simplex through transformed Gaussian coordinates \\citep{AitchisonShen1980}. Log-ratio analysis addresses the dependence imposed by closure; ILR coordinates provide an orthonormal representation of its contrast space \\citep{Aitchison1982,Egozcue2003,Pawlowsky2011}. We use this representation as a working geometry, not as evidence that all simplex distributions become well approximated by ellipsoids. In particular, strictly positive Dirichlet responses can lie close to the boundary, where log ratios are highly sensitive to small components.\n\n\\paragraph{Calibration and adaptation.}\nSplit-conformal regression allows a flexible fitted score while retaining marginal calibration \\citep{Lei2018}. Conformalized quantile regression adapts interval width to heteroskedasticity \\citep{Romano2019}; our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling. Cross-validation and predictive sample reuse motivate held-out residual estimation \\citep{Stone1974,Geisser1975}, but training residual cross-fitting is not itself a conformal procedure. In particular, the present construction is not jackknife+: that method combines leave-one-out predictions and residuals in its region construction \\citep{Barber2021Jackknife}. Our separate calibration sample remains essential.\n\n\\paragraph{Coverage targets and evaluation.}\nConditional validity is stronger than marginal validity, and distribution-free conditional inference faces fundamental limitations \\citep{Vovk2012,Barber2021Limits}. Label-adaptive and class-conditional classification methods address distinct targets from coverage of an observed continuous composition \\citep{Romano2020,Ding2023}. Weighted conformal methods and extensions beyond exchangeability address distribution shift with additional structure \\citep{Tibshirani2019,Barber2023}; we do not implement those corrections. Instead, shift is an explicit stress test. Finally, our coverage--volume objective is not a proper scoring rule for a predictive distribution \\citep{Gneiting2007}. Reporting its components separately is necessary to distinguish empirical compactness from reliability.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem Setup and Design Rationale}\nLet $X\\in\\R^5$ and $Y\\in\\simplex=\\{y\\in(0,1)^K:\\sum_j y_j=1\\}$. The target is a region $C_\\alpha(X)$ containing a future observed composition with probability at least $1-\\alpha$, marginally under an exchangeable calibration/test law. The evaluated target is $\\alpha=0.1$. We study joint membership of the entire response vector; neither componentwise intervals nor coverage of a latent distribution is the estimand. The empirical trade-off is test coverage versus normalized volume under the uniform distribution on the simplex.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L34-L45]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\nThe working hypothesis is that a pooled residual norm wastes volume when residual scales vary predictably with $X$. Training-derived scale and shape adjustments may align the region with this heterogeneity before calibration. Boundary-dependent shapes are a limited proxy for variation in residual geometry, not a guarantee of local validity. The countervailing hypothesis is that log-ratio tail behavior or covariate shift can defeat compactness or coverage despite this adaptation. These hypotheses concern end-to-end configurations: the experiment does not isolate the causal contribution of every fitted component.\n\n\\subsection{Reference Geometries}\nChoose $B\\in\\R^{K\\times(K-1)}$ with $B^TB=I$ and $B^T\\mathbf{1}=0$. Write $z(y)=B^T\\log y$ and $h(z)=\\operatorname{softmax}(Bz)$. All location models use a quadratic design $\\phi(x)$ containing an intercept, each input, its square, and distinct pairwise products. Ridge fits use penalty $0.01$, including the intercept \\citep{HoerlKennard1970}. The Euclidean reference fits responses directly, clips fitted components below $10^{-12}$, renormalizes, and uses $s_E(x,y)=\\|y-\\widehat p(x)\\|_2$. The ILR reference fits $z(Y)$ and uses $s_I(x,y)=\\|z(y)-\\widehat m_I(x)\\|_2$. Both references fit locations on the first three quarters of the available training rows. Their calibrated sets are respectively simplex-intersected Euclidean balls and inverse-ILR images of isotropic balls.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L624-L680]]\n\n\\subsection{Training-Adaptive ILR Residual Geometry}\nThe studied adaptive configuration partitions training rows deterministically into four folds by row index modulo four. Each row receives a residual $r_i=z(y_i)-\\widehat m^{(-f(i))}(x_i)$ from a ridge location fit excluding its fold. A final location $\\widehat m$ is refitted on all training rows. For each ILR coordinate, a second ridge regression with penalty $5$ fits $\\log(r_{ij}^2+0.02)$ on $\\phi(x_i)$. With fitted value $v_j(x)$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n D(x)=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sigma_j(x)),\\qquad\n \\sigma_j(x)=\\exp\\!\\left\\{\\tfrac12\\clip(v_j(x),-6,5)\\right\\}.\n \\label{eq:scale}\n\\end{equation}\nThe standardized residual vector $u_i=D(x_i)^{-1}r_i$ is clipped coordinatewise to $[-1000,1000]$. These safeguards are fixed numerical choices, not independently optimized or ablated findings.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L90-L123]]\n\nBoundary proximity is summarized by $b(x)=\\min_j h(\\widehat m(x))_j$. Training predictions define its lower and upper tertiles; right-sided threshold assignment gives a group $g(x)$. For dimension $d=K-1$, the fitted second-moment shapes are\n\\begin{equation}\n S_0=\\frac{\\sum_i u_i u_i^T+8I_d}{n_{\\rm tr}+8},\\qquad\n S_g=\\frac{\\sum_{i:g(x_i)=g}u_i u_i^T+32S_0}{n_g+32}.\n \\label{eq:shape}\n\\end{equation}\nLet $H_gH_g^T=S_g$ be its Cholesky factor and $L(x)=D(x)H_{g(x)}$. The adaptive score is\n\\begin{equation}\n s_A(x,y)=\\left\\|L(x)^{-1}\\{z(y)-\\widehat m(x)\\}\\right\\|_2.\n \\label{eq:score}\n\\end{equation}\nThese are uncentered residual second moments, not unbiased sample covariances. Shrinkage toward a positive target stabilizes the shapes, in the broad spirit of covariance regularization \\citep{SchaferStrimmer2005,Chen2010}; this is not an implementation of either cited estimator. All group boundaries, scales, and factors are frozen before calibration. Only the residual geometry is group-dependent: no groupwise conformal quantiles are fitted.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L116-L131]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L190-L198]]\n\n\\subsection{Independent Calibration and Guarantee Scope}\nFor any one of the frozen scores, sort its $n_{\\rm cal}$ calibration values and select rank $k=\\lceil(n_{\\rm cal}+1)(1-\\alpha)\\rceil$. Use $q=+\\infty$ if $k>n_{\\rm cal}$; otherwise use the $k$th value. The region is $C_\\alpha(x)=\\{y\\in\\simplex:s(x,y)\\le q\\}$. Conditional on a fixed training fit, exchangeability of the calibration and future scores gives the standard rank argument:\n\\begin{equation}\n \\Pr\\{Y_{\\rm new}\\in C_\\alpha(X_{\\rm new})\\}\\ge 1-\\alpha.\n \\label{eq:coverage}\n\\end{equation}\nWith no ties the rank is uniform among the calibration-plus-test scores; ties with the nonrandomized inclusion rule make the bound conservative. This is the usual split-conformal argument \\citep{Lei2018,Angelopoulos2021}, not a new theorem. It does not lower-bound every realized finite-test coverage fraction, require Gaussian residuals, or establish conditional coverage. Under test-only covariate shift the exchangeability premise fails. Training cross-fitting can affect efficiency, but does not repair that premise.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L859-L864]]\n\n\\section{Experimental Protocol}\n\\subsection{Synthetic Conditions and Comparators}\nThe released study covers a single composition-regression task, with $K=3$ or $8$ and five Gaussian covariates. Four response conditions are paired with two information-removal controls. The homoskedastic condition (C1) uses a quadratic ILR mean and Gaussian noise with scale $0.35$. The heteroskedastic condition (C2) multiplies that scale by $\\exp\\{0.65\\clip(x_1,-3,3)\\}$ and coordinate-specific factors from $0.5$ to $1.5$. These two conditions have $K=3$. The boundary condition (C3) has $K=8$ and uses a Dirichlet response with a shifted ILR mean and low concentrations. The shifted condition (C4), also with $K=8$, uses a different Dirichlet concentration and modifies test covariates only: $x_1$ increases by $1.5$ and $x_2$ is multiplied by $1.5$. Appendix~\\ref{sec:generators} gives the precise generator. All responses are strictly positive; structural zeros are not evaluated.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L776-L814]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/final_resource_manifest.json]]\n\nThe constant-input control (C5) retains C2 responses but sets every training, calibration, and test covariate to zero. The unpaired-training control (C6) uses C2 data but cyclically shifts training covariate rows, leaving training responses and all calibration/test pairs unchanged. These are matched information-removal comparisons, not independent dataset families. Their inclusion in an aggregate intentionally gives additional weight to the C2 response family.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1007-L1036]]\n\nEach held-out seed uses 4096 training, 2048 calibration, and 3840 test observations per condition. The five seed labels are 1009, 2027, 3049, 4051, and 5059. Each method sees the same data within a condition and has its own independently computed score threshold from the shared calibration split; the selected rank is 1845. The adaptive configuration was frozen using a separate development corpus with three seeds. Alternative adaptive configurations are shown only on that development corpus in the supplement, not relabeled as held-out replications. No further experiment was run for this manuscript.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#all_programs]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/final_resource_manifest.json]]\n\nThe principal comparison is between fitted systems rather than training-size-matched geometry interventions. In particular, the reference locations use only three quarters of the training observations, whereas the adaptive location uses all of them after residual cross-fitting. The single-split adaptive comparison also changes residual allocation and location refitting together. We therefore do not infer that any difference is caused solely by cross-fitting, boundary grouping, or covariance adjustment.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L665-L672]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/single_split.py#L108-L130]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/adaptive.py#L108-L130]]\n\n\\subsection{Common-Measure Volume and Uncertainty}\nDevelopment selection used a fixed utility that penalizes a shortfall from nominal coverage and the volume fraction: $U(\\widehat c,\\widehat V)=1-0.8\\max(0,0.9-\\widehat c)/0.9-0.2\\widehat V$. The objective averages across the specified conditions and seeds. It is not a constrained optimization requiring coverage to meet the target: a sufficiently compact region can offset a coverage shortfall in this utility. Conversely, coverage above the target receives no extra reward. The configuration selected by this objective therefore requires a separate scientific assessment of its components. We do not identify selection under that trade-off with satisfaction of every performance target. All observed conditions, including those unfavorable to the frozen configuration, remain in the final comparison.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L932-L938]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1245-L1263]]\n\nAt each of 64 evenly indexed test anchors, the evaluator draws 4096 shared probes from $\\operatorname{Dirichlet}(1,\\ldots,1)$. For region $C$ and $A$ anchors with $M$ probes each,\n\\begin{equation}\n \\widehat V(C)=\\frac1A\\sum_{a=1}^A\\widehat p_a,\n \\qquad \\widehat p_a=\\frac1M\\sum_{m=1}^M\\mathbf1\\{U_{am}\\in C(x_a)\\}.\n \\label{eq:volume}\n\\end{equation}\nUniform simplex probes estimate a normalized raw-simplex volume fraction for every method. ILR radii and ellipsoid determinants are not substituted for this common measure. Identical probes within a condition support paired integration-error estimates. Volume remains anchor-dependent; its reported integration error does not quantify uncertainty from choosing anchors or fitting a model.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L882-L938]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1033-L1036]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1206-L1225]]\n\nWe show all seed-level observations and their means. Retained Wilson intervals describe finite-test coverage uncertainty \\citep{Brown2001}; retained Monte Carlo standard errors describe volume integration conditional on the fitted sets and anchors. For aggregate contrasts, the evaluator resamples the five seed blocks 2000 times, preserving all six conditions together, and reports percentile intervals \\citep{Efron1979,Davison1997}. These few-seed intervals are exploratory and not simultaneous. They must not be repurposed as per-condition intervals. Four overlapping masks, $x_1<0$, $x_1\\ge0$, $x_2<0$, and $x_2\\ge0$, provide descriptive reliability diagnostics; they are distinct from training-defined boundary groups.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L871-L880]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1266-L1294]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{A Useful Heteroskedastic Trade-off, Not Uniform Dominance}\nTable~\\ref{tab:means} and Figure~\\ref{fig:comparison} compare both target quantities across every held-out condition. In the heteroskedastic condition, adaptive ILR attains mean coverage 0.9044 at volume 0.1476, compared with 0.9030/0.3336 for Euclidean balls and 0.9034/0.3359 for isotropic ILR balls. Thus the adaptive configuration produces substantially smaller measured regions at similar observed marginal coverage in this condition. The individual-seed displays show that this is not merely a favorable aggregate hiding a volume reversal across seeds. The result supports the tested configuration's efficiency in this setting, not population dominance or isolated attribution to its shape model.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\input{tables/condition_means.tex}\n\\caption{Held-out means over five seeds. Volume is a fraction of the same simplex measure for all methods, not an ILR-space volume. ``Constant'' and ``Unpaired'' are C2-derived controls. The target coverage is 0.90; means are descriptive, not condition-specific confidence bounds.}\n\\label{tab:means}\n\\end{table}\n\nIn the homoskedastic condition, adaptive ILR and the ILR ball have almost identical mean volumes, 0.1286 and 0.1284, with coverage 0.8983 and 0.8991. Both are smaller than the Euclidean region, whose volume is 0.1782. This contrast is important: adaptation is not uniformly useful even within the logistic-normal family. Approximate constancy of residual scale reduces the motivation for a more elaborate geometry, although the end-to-end comparison cannot identify that explanation causally.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\begin{subfigure}{\\textwidth}\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,trim=0 136.8 0 0,clip]{figures/coverage_volume_by_regime.pdf}\n\\caption{Joint response coverage.}\\label{fig:coverage}\n\\end{subfigure}\n\\begin{subfigure}{\\textwidth}\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth,trim=0 0 0 136.8,clip]{figures/coverage_volume_by_regime.pdf}\n\\caption{Common-simplex volume fraction.}\\label{fig:volume}\n\\end{subfigure}\n\\caption{Condition-specific coverage and common-simplex volume. Small dots are the five held-out seeds; horizontal marks are their means. The dashed line denotes nominal coverage, not a confidence bound. The dotted separator distinguishes response conditions from matched information-removal controls. Compactness and coverage must be read together: shifted Euclidean regions are smaller but under-cover, while boundary ILR regions nearly fill the simplex.}\n\\label{fig:comparison}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Boundary Inefficiency and Covariate-Shift Failure}\nThe boundary-concentrated Dirichlet condition reverses the favorable efficiency story. Adaptive ILR has volume 0.9759 and coverage 0.8947. The isotropic ILR ball has volume 0.999998, effectively the full simplex at the reported resolution, whereas the Euclidean ball uses 0.7155 with coverage 0.8992. The adaptive shape reduces volume relative to isotropic ILR but does not make the region compact relative to the raw-simplex reference. A near-full-simplex region can achieve unremarkable coverage on a boundary-concentrated response distribution because uniform simplex volume is not response probability mass. This is a limitation of the tested score/geometry combination, not an integration-based proof of good uncertainty quality.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\nUnder test-only covariate shift, adaptive coverage is 0.8633, below the 0.90 target. It exceeds the ILR ball's 0.7082 but is close to the Euclidean ball's 0.8592. Its volume is 0.6221, versus 0.6145 for the ILR ball and 0.1928 for Euclidean balls. The shift therefore does not yield a clean efficiency advantage: adaptive ILR is substantially larger than Euclidean while both under-cover. The known change in the test covariate law removes the premise of the ordinary calibration argument. The measurements do not isolate whether each shortfall is driven by location extrapolation, scale extrapolation, or residual-shape mismatch.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json]]\n\n\\subsection{Local Diagnostics and Information-Removal Controls}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:strata} shows that marginal adaptation does not remove local imbalance. In the heteroskedastic condition, adaptive mean coverage is 0.9740 for $x_1<0$ and 0.8339 for $x_1\\ge0$; the corresponding ILR-ball rates are 0.9975 and 0.8082. The adaptive score moderates this contrast but leaves substantial undercoverage in the higher-noise half. These are overlapping sign-mask summaries across seeds, not independent partitions or simultaneous conditional confidence statements. No evaluated boundary-tertile coverage guarantee can be inferred from them.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/descriptive_sign_strata.pdf}\n\\caption{Descriptive sign-stratum reliability in heteroskedastic and shifted conditions. Dots show each held-out seed and bars show means. Masks for different covariates overlap; their coverage rates are not simultaneous guarantees. The nominal line is a reference only. No empty-mask zero encodings are plotted.}\n\\label{fig:strata}\n\\end{figure}\n\nRemoving informative covariates produces wide adaptive regions: mean volume is 0.6932 for constant inputs and 0.7004 after training-pair derangement, compared with 0.1476 in the intact heteroskedastic condition. Their adaptive coverage remains approximately nominal at 0.9019 and 0.9023. This shows the efficiency contrast depends on accessible training information; it does not certify any particular information-extraction mechanism. The constant-input case also exposes a reporting artifact. The retained worst-stratum summary is 0.0 because empty negative-sign masks are encoded as zero. It is not an observed subgroup with zero coverage. We preserve the original summary and its limitation rather than silently substituting a different statistic; per-mask counts are not retained in the episode records.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.worst_stratum_coverage]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L924-L928]]\n\n\\subsection{Aggregate Comparisons and Completed Checks}\nThe equal-weight six-condition adaptive averages are coverage 0.8942 and volume 0.5446. Aggregate paired contrasts in Table~\\ref{tab:contrasts} favor adaptive ILR over the isotropic ILR reference in both coverage and volume, but not over Euclidean in volume. In particular, the Euclidean volume-reduction interval is entirely negative. This aggregate includes correlated C2-derived controls and the shifted condition, so it is not a population marginal coverage estimate or an estimate over six independent task families. The separate condition results, rather than this summary, establish the paper's central trade-off.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.marginal_coverage]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.normalized_volume]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{llrr}\n\\toprule\nComparator & Contrast & Mean & Paired bootstrap 95\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nEuclidean & Coverage: adaptive minus reference & $-0.0014$ & $[-0.0065,0.0039]$ \\\\\nEuclidean & Volume: reference minus adaptive & $-0.0346$ & $[-0.0546,-0.0115]$ \\\\\nILR ball & Coverage: adaptive minus reference & $0.0251$ & $[0.0185,0.0319]$ \\\\\nILR ball & Volume: reference minus adaptive & $0.0496$ & $[0.0306,0.0618]$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Equal-weight contrasts across all six conditions. Positive values favor adaptive ILR. Intervals resample five paired seed blocks and retain all conditions together; few-seed percentile intervals are unstable and not multiplicity-adjusted. They are not per-condition error bars.}\n\\label{tab:contrasts}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage_gap_vs_euclidean]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.volume_gain_vs_euclidean]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage_gap_vs_ilr]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.volume_gain_vs_ilr]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe final evaluation completed and satisfied its recorded qualification checks. Observed checks include query-order/subset invariance, singleton/full-simplex membership controls, matched interventions, and a replay. Their scope is operational: they do not establish conditional validity, shift robustness, a comprehensive nonadaptivity audit, or universal superiority. No unreported diagnostic is inferred from the qualification outcome. The performance failures above remain scientific limitations notwithstanding completion of these checks.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.final.qualification.status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.final.qualification.outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#grounding_sources.final.qualification.correct]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
