{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat > postdraft_reference_query_plan.json <<'EOF'\n{\n  \\\"status\\\": \\\"ready\\\",\n  \\\"central_topic\\\": \\\"Paragraph-specific positioning of training-adaptive ILR ellipsoids for continuous compositional regression against joint multivariate conformal prediction regions with ellipsoidal residual geometry or covariate-dependent residual scaling.\\\",\n  \\\"core_methods\\\": [\n    \\\"Training-adaptive ILR residual ellipsoids with covariate-dependent diagonal scales and training-defined boundary-group shapes, followed by independent split-conformal calibration.\\\",\n    \\\"Joint membership of the observed multivariate response, not separate marginal intervals or coverage of a latent probability vector.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"comparators\\\": [\n    \\\"Literature-only target: joint multivariate split-conformal regions using ellipsoidal residual geometry or residual covariance.\\\",\n    \\\"Literature-only target: locally adaptive joint multivariate conformal regions using covariate-dependent residual scaling.\\\",\n    \\\"The evaluated Euclidean and isotropic ILR balls remain the experimental references; retrieved methods would be positioning comparators, not evaluated baselines.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"benchmarks\\\": [],\n  \\\"architectural_shifts\\\": [],\n  \\\"result_driven_themes\\\": [],\n  \\\"queries\\\": [\n    {\n      \\\"query\\\": \\\"split-conformal multivariate regression ellipsoidal prediction regions residual covariance\\\",\n      \\\"category\\\": \\\"baseline_comparator\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"High-priority gap related-work-joint-region-comparators, Related Work, Calibration and adaptation paragraph (paper.tex:58), anchored at 'our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling'. Seek direct joint-region positioning for ellipsoidal geometry rather than another general conformal or covariance-shrinkage reference. Lei2018 supports split calibration, while SchaferStrimmer2005 and Chen2010 support shape stabilization; none is identified by the gap artifacts or existing provenance as the dedicated joint-region comparator needed here. Before any later citation, verify joint response coverage, the residual geometry and covariance construction, and separation of fitting from calibration.\\\"\n    },\n    {\n      \\\"query\\\": \\\"locally adaptive conformal multivariate prediction regions covariate-dependent residual scaling\\\",\n      \\\"category\\\": \\\"baseline_comparator\\\",\n      \\\"rationale\\\": \\\"The same high-priority target and anchor in Related Work (paper.tex:58) contrast scalar CQR (Romano2019) with joint residual rescaling without directly positioning adaptive multivariate regions. Seek complementary evidence on input-dependent residual scale and its calibration, not another scalar interval or conditional-validity survey. Inspect whether the construction covers the full response jointly, what varies with covariates, and which coverage assumptions are stated. Deduplicate against the first query and all 25 existing bibliography/provenance entries; one source may satisfy both directions. Do not infer ILR coordinates, boundary routing, or conditional validity from local adaptation.\\\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \\\"forbidden_query_patterns\\\": [\n    \\\"Broad conformal-prediction, compositional-data, logistic-normal, or ILR literature refreshes; those foundations already have direct citations.\\\",\n    \\\"Repeated title or identifier lookups for existing bibliography entries, or near-duplicate searches that add no joint-region positioning evidence.\\\",\n    \\\"New benchmark or dataset searches, real-data applications, structural zeros, alternative transforms, Hellinger scores, or extra calibration schemes.\\\",\n    \\\"Queries framing jackknife+, groupwise or Mondrian calibration, importance weighting, or newly found joint-region methods as evaluated procedures in this study.\\\",\n    \\\"Queries premised on universal superiority, guaranteed conditional coverage, demonstrated interpolation improvement, or covariate-shift robustness.\\\",\n    \\\"Expanding beyond the single Calibration and adaptation paragraph gap or seeking more than 1-3 directly relevant new sources across both queries.\\\"\n  ],\n  \\\"failure_reason\\\": \\\"\\\"\n}\nEOF\ncat > postdraft_reference_query_plan.md <<'EOF'\n# Post-Draft Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready\n\n## Central Topic and Grounding\n\nPosition the manuscript's training-adaptive ILR ellipsoids against joint multivariate conformal prediction regions with ellipsoidal residual geometry or covariate-dependent residual scaling. Read \"'`draft_reference_gaps.md` and `draft_reference_gaps.json` first, then checked the draft, original query-plan schema, existing bibliography, provenance, and global memory summary. Memory supplies qualitative context only, not numerical evidence.\n\nThe gap artifacts identify exactly one high-priority positioning target: `related-work-joint-region-comparators`, in Related Work'\"'s **Calibration and adaptation** paragraph (\"'`paper.tex:58`). Its anchor is “our study instead evaluates joint multivariate membership after residual rescaling”. The study fits adaptive ILR residual geometry on training data and calibrates the joint region separately. This plan does not propose changes to that construction.\n\n## Incremental Scope\n\nUse **two complementary queries**, deliberately below the default range because the sole supported gap supplies only these two focused directions. Seek **1–3 directly relevant new sources total**, not per query; one source can address both directions. Stop when joint geometry and adaptation are adequately positioned in this paragraph. Do not pad the query set or refresh the bibliography.\n\nAll 25 bibliography entries have matching provenance entries. Existing coverage includes general split-conformal regression (`Lei2018`), scalar conformalized quantile regression (`Romano2019`), residual reuse (`Stone1974`, `Geisser1975`, `Barber2021Jackknife`), and covariance stabilization (`SchaferStrimmer2005`, `Chen2010`). These do not fill the dedicated joint-region positioning gap identified by the memo. Citation presence and recorded metadata/abstract reading scope are not independent full-text verification.\n\n## Queries in Priority Order\n\n### 1. Joint Ellipsoidal Geometry\n\n- **Query:** `split-conformal multivariate regression ellipsoidal prediction regions residual covariance`\n- **Category:** `baseline_comparator`\n- **Target:** `related-work-joint-region-comparators`; Related Work, Calibration and adaptation; `paper.tex:58`, at the anchor above.\n- **Rationale:** Supply a direct joint-region comparator for the transition from scalar CQR to multivariate residual membership. General split calibration and covariance-shrinkage citations cover different roles and should not be retrieved again.\n- **Later verification:** Check joint response coverage, ellipsoidal score/region geometry, residual covariance construction, and separation of fitting from calibration before asserting a relationship to the manuscript.\n\n### 2. Covariate-Dependent Residual Scaling\n\n- **Query:** `locally adaptive conformal multivariate prediction regions covariate-dependent residual scaling`\n- **Category:** `baseline_comparator`\n- **Target:** The same paragraph, target identifier, and literal anchor as Query 1.\n- **Rationale:** Position adaptive joint residual rescaling beyond the already-cited scalar CQR example. This complements the geometry query rather than opening a new literature family or rewrite target.\n- **Later verification:** Confirm joint rather than componentwise coverage, identify what depends on covariates, and inspect the calibration construction and stated coverage assumptions. Local adaptation alone does not imply conditional validity. Deduplicate against Query 1 and all existing bibliography/provenance entries.\n\n## Context Fields and Exclusions\n\nBoth queries concern literature-only positioning comparators. The evaluated Euclidean and isotropic ILR balls remain the experimental references; no newly found method becomes an experimental baseline. The JSON'\"'s \"'`core_methods` and `comparators` fields capture this context. Its `benchmarks`, `architectural_shifts`, and `result_driven_themes` lists are empty because the gap analysis supports no retrieval in those directions.\n\nDo not search broadly for conformal foundations, composition geometry, logistic-normal models, ILR, covariance regularization, or uncertainty summaries already cited. Exclude new datasets, real-data applications, structural zeros, alternative transforms, Hellinger scores, and extra calibration schemes. Do not portray jackknife+, groupwise/Mondrian calibration, importance weighting, or retrieved comparator methods as executed here. Do not imply superiority over untested methods, conditional validity, shift robustness, a demonstrated interpolation advantage, or prior use of this study'\"'s ILR transform or boundary routing.\n\n## Bounded Follow-Up and Stop Rule\n\nA later retrieval stage may inspect and deduplicate candidates, verify their actual constructions, and support a brief comparison of joint geometry, adaptation, and separate calibration in the identified paragraph. Retain at most three directly relevant new sources across both queries and stop once those positioning needs are met. If candidates do not support a comparison, report the unresolved gap rather than inventing a citation or broadening the search.\n\nNo references were retrieved in this stage. The manuscript, bibliography, reference provenance, gap artifacts, global memory summary, and original query plan remain unchanged.\nEOF\npython - <<'PY'\nimport json\nimport re\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\noriginal = json.loads(Path('reference_query_plan.json').read_text())\nplan = json.loads(Path('postdraft_reference_query_plan.json').read_text())\ngaps = json.loads(Path('draft_reference_gaps.json').read_text())\nprovenance = json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nmemo = Path('postdraft_reference_query_plan.md').read_text()\ndraft = Path('paper.tex').read_text()\nallowed = {'core_method', 'baseline_comparator', 'benchmark_context', 'architectural_shift', 'result_driven_theme'}\nassert set(plan) == set(original), 'Top-level schema differs'\nassert all(type(plan[key]) is type(original[key]) for key in original), 'Field types differ'\nassert plan['status'] == 'ready' and plan['central_topic'].strip() and plan['queries']\nassert plan['failure_reason'] == ''\nassert gaps['status'] == 'ready' and len(gaps['rewrite_targets']) == 1\nassert len(plan['queries']) == 2\nassert len({item['query'] for item in plan['queries']}) == 2\nfor item in plan['queries']:\n    assert set(item) == set(original['queries'][0])\n    assert isinstance(item['query'], str) and item['query'].strip()\n    assert isinstance(item['rationale'], str) and item['rationale'].strip()\n    assert item['category'] in allowed\n    assert item['query'] in memo and item['category'] in memo\n    assert gaps['rewrite_targets'][0]['target_id'] in item['rationale'] or 'same high-priority target' in item['rationale']\nassert gaps['rewrite_targets'][0]['anchor_text'] in draft\nassert gaps['rewrite_targets'][0]['anchor_text'] in memo\nbib_keys = set(re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*([\"'^,]+),'\"', Path('references.bib').read_text()))\nprovenance_keys = {item['citation_key'] for item in provenance['references']}\nassert len(bib_keys) == 25 and bib_keys == provenance_keys\nprint('PASS: matching plan schema, 2 grounded queries, valid categories, memo parity, draft anchor, and 25 bibliography/provenance keys.')\nPY\nsha256sum -c /tmp/postdraft_reference_plan_input_hashes\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
